;; talk/kernel--person.scm — objections to kernel.scm and apps.scm from the lens of the person.

(objection 'o-kernel-person-1 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'k-coverage :lens "person"
  :claim (canon 'keep-evidence "The coverage screen answers 'if I lose this device today, which counterparties could I still leave alone?' and offers nothing. By the kernel's own design it says 'not covered' on native TRON and perhaps for every board, so on the worst day a person is told, per counterparty, that they are unprotected, with no step to take and no way to change it. The screen shows no figures either, so it cannot say how much money sits behind each 'not covered'. Nothing in the kernel says what a person on TRON should do instead: keep a second device, export a copy, or not put money there. The person learns the bad news while the phone is still working and has nothing to do with it, and on the day the phone is lost the same screen cannot help. Cost: a warning that cannot be acted on is read once and then ignored, and the person who most needs the copy is the one the kernel cannot give it to."))

(objection 'o-kernel-person-2 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'd-kernel-app-door :lens "person"
  :claim (guess "The leaning makes letting a program act one yes, and then every proposal the program makes is signed at once inside the face's standing rules. So the one question the person is asked, 'let this program act?', is in fact 'let it spend everything my limits allow, in any counterparty, at any hour, for as long as I do not stop it'. The person cannot judge that from a program's name, and the kernel gives no per-program cap, recipient list or expiry; scoped grants are the option the leaning set aside. A person who trusts a charting program with one pair has also trusted it with every limit they set for their own hand. The listing of what each program caused arrives after the signatures. Cost: the person is asked a question they cannot answer, and the answer they give is wider than the one they meant."))

(objection 'o-kernel-person-3 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'k-attention :lens "person"
  :claim (protocol "core/watchtower/dispute-watch.ts" "The kernel gives the person one list of everything with a deadline, and says in its own text that reaching the person outside the program does not exist yet. Only the wake on leaving is built, and only where the watching service works. Payments in flight, fee rises under notice, skipped collateral requests and the end of a leaving window have deadlines that run while the phone is in a pocket. The protocol's windows are long and measured in hours (twenty-five by default, law exit-timing), so a person who cannot be awake at the end of one is asked to come back at a set time with nothing that calls them. Cost: an ordinary person who looks at the list once a week finds the item on the day it has already cost them, and the list is a record of what was missed more often than a warning."))

(objection 'o-kernel-person-4 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'd-kernel-programs :lens "person"
  :claim (canon 'entity-is "Each entity is opened in exactly one face, and a face shows nothing of the others. The kernel itself says a trader may also be a private person (d-kernel-words), and a person's work changes over a life: someone who pays friends starts to trade, a shop becomes a hub, a one-person firm adds a board. An entity is the person or organisation with its accounts and its collateral, so changing face cannot be a change of view; it is either impossible, or it means a new entity, new accounts and new money locked while the old ones are wound down. The kernel names no way to change face and no way to hold two. Cost: the person who outgrows their first face has to leave every counterparty and start again, or live in a face that does not fit; the design never says which."))

(objection 'o-kernel-person-5 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'k-open-flow :lens "person"
  :claim (canon 'auto-top-up-default "At opening, the flow states every setting the opening put in place, in money and hours, and asks the person to agree. The settings are an automatic collateral request with a trigger and a fee ceiling, a public listing and a response time, and the person has no way to judge any of them on the first day: they do not know what a reasonable trigger is, how long a company should be allowed to take to answer, or what being listed costs them. A yes to a list of numbers one cannot weigh is a signature, not a choice, and the second signature that applies the collateral setting is one more step they cannot evaluate. Cost: the person is asked at the moment of least knowledge, agrees to move on, and finds out what the settings meant on the first day they fire."))

(objection 'o-kernel-person-6 :by "sonnet-person" :to 'd-kernel-secured :lens "person"
  :claim (canon 'collateral-secured "The leaning calls the locked share secured whatever its size and puts the cost of leaving in a separate figure beside it. A person with a few dollars locked, against a charge of twenty to collect it, sees the word 'secured' and a number, and the word is what they will remember: secured means safe. The protocol's meaning is exact, but the person does not read a protocol; they read a word, and the fixed words of d-kernel-words are fixed so that nobody misreads risk. Here the fixed word invites the misreading in the other direction, on small accounts, which is most of a private person's accounts. Cost: a person feels covered by money that nobody would pay the chain to collect, and learns it on the worst day, when leaving costs more than it returns."))
